WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.810
The Invisible Man was a fairly popular movie
before quarantine. I wonder why this episode

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.560
of Things You Missed is struggling.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.050
People
are watching, the ratings are good, I wonder

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.480
what could be the issue… Let’s look at
the comments.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.010
“Pretty sure Screen Rent ripped this off.
Practically word for word.”

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.020
Yeah that’s an issue.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.880
On February 27th 2020, I uploaded a new episode
of my long running web-series Things You Missed,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.419
a show where I analyse the often overlooked
details of your favorite horror movies. When

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:33.730
my videos started to get popular in 2017,
I noticed a lot of imitators, which is understandable.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.730
Chasing trends is pretty common in this era
of YouTube, and it makes sense that other

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.970
YouTubers might have their own interpretations
of horror films. And I wouldn’t really be

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.540
bothered if another creator came to a similar
conclusion about what a director intended

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.511
with his or her movie from time to time. It
only makes sense. So I usually ignore these

00:00:47.511 --> 00:00:49.080
knock-off videos when I see them.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.720
Then I saw this comment. Practically word
for word? That got my attention. On March

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.980
6th, over a week after I published my video,
Screen Rant, a massive company with over 7

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:03.380
million subscribers compared to my 350K at
the time, puts out 25 Things You Missed in

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:06.479
The Invisible Man. Usually when I see these
knock-off videos, they at least change the

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:10.229
title up a bit like they’ll say Small Details
You Missed, or Things You Didn’t Notice.

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:12.760
But Screen Rant decided to straight up steal
my title.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.880
Not the end of the world. Let’s compare
the actual videos. Here’s a clip from my

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.850
video:
“Adrian’s full name by the way is Adrian

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.729
Griffin, which is a slight nod to Jack Griffin,
the scientist who learned how to make himself

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:25.440
invisible and became the original invisible
man.”

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.660
And he’s the Screen Rant video:

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.140
But this isn’t the first time
we’ve seen a disappearing man with the last

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.630
name Griffin. Way back in the 1933 version
of The Invisible Man, we saw the character

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.899
Jack Griffin played by Claude Rains.”

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:42.560
